# 8. QR Code Security

Use cryptographically secure random tokens.

Recommended token:
- At least 256 bits of random entropy.
- URL-safe Base64 or hexadecimal representation.
- Store only a secure hash of the token in MySQL.
- Return the original token only when generated.
- Do not use sequential video IDs in public URLs.

Example viewing URL:

`https://your-domain.com/v/UNIQUE_RANDOM_TOKEN`

The QR link acts as a bearer credential.

Anyone possessing the link can access the associated video while it remains available.

No Gmail login or guest registration is required.

The QR page must:
- Display only the associated video.
- Support mobile playback.
- Provide a download button.
- Display an expired-link message after expiration.
- Never expose other event videos.
- Avoid third-party advertising or unnecessary trackers.

Use HTTPS everywhere.

Apply request rate limiting to public token endpoints.

Prevent token enumeration and use constant-time token hash comparison where applicable.

# 9. Video Viewing Website

Build a lightweight, mobile-responsive web experience.

The video page should contain:

- RetroTok branding
- Video player
- Play/pause controls
- Fullscreen playback
- Download button
- Event name
- Expiration notice
- Expired/unavailable state

Use native HTML5 video playback.

The actual MP4 remains in private R2 storage.

When a guest opens the page, the backend validates the token and creates a short-lived signed playback URL.

Recommended playback URL expiration: approximately 15–60 minutes, configurable.

A new signed URL should be issued when the guest reopens the QR page.

The QR token remains valid for the 30-day retention period, not for the duration of the signed playback URL.

# 10. Local Storage and Upload Recovery

This is a critical requirement because the actual kiosk runs Android 6.0.1/API 23 and has limited storage.

Rules:

1. Never delete a recording before cloud verification.
2. Never automatically delete a pending upload.
3. Never delete an interrupted or recoverable recording.
4. Maintain a persistent upload queue.
5. Resume or restart incomplete uploads safely.
6. Use file streaming rather than loading MP4 files into memory.
7. Allow only one active upload at a time.
8. Reconcile local upload state with backend state after application restart.
9. Retain a local thumbnail after successful upload if storage permits.
10. Delete the local MP4 only after cloud verification and successful database finalization.

After successful upload, Home should use the cloud video URL for playback.

Implement a small, size-limited playback cache. The cache must never delete pending uploads or unpublished recordings.

Reuse the existing StorageHealth implementation:
- Warn at the existing low-storage warning threshold.
- Block new recordings at the existing critical threshold.
- Show the admin the number and size of pending uploads.
- Require administrator action when storage is insufficient.

If local storage reaches the critical threshold:
- Block new recording.
- Do not silently discard any file.
- Notify the administrator.
- Provide upload retry and safe cleanup tools.

# 11. Internet Failure Handling

Recording and processing must continue without internet access.

| Situation | Required behavior |
|---|---|
| Internet unavailable before recording | Allow recording |
| Internet unavailable during processing | Preserve completed local video |
| Internet unavailable during upload | Mark pending/retry |
| Upload URL expired | Request a new URL |
| Upload interrupted | Retry safely |
| Backend unavailable | Keep local queue |
| R2 unavailable | Keep local queue |
| App restarted | Reconcile pending uploads |
| Kiosk restarted | Recover valid local recordings |
| Upload succeeds | Verify before local cleanup |

Use exponential backoff with a maximum retry delay and avoid aggressive repeated network requests.

A failed upload must never block browsing existing videos.

# 12. Event Lifecycle Integration

Reuse the existing Phase 7 EventLifecycleManager.

Do not create a separate event lifecycle.

Event states should support:
- ACTIVE
- PAUSED
- CLOSING
- ENDED

Manual event ending:

1. Admin chooses End Event.
2. Block new recordings.
3. Allow active recording/composition to finish safely.
4. Preserve pending uploads.
5. Continue uploading videos belonging to the event.
6. Mark the event ENDED.
7. Keep its online videos available until their individual expiration dates.

An event ending must not cancel an already initiated upload or delete pending files.

The backend must accept completion of an upload session that was validly initiated while the event was active, even if the event is subsequently ended.

# 13. Thirty-Day Retention Policy

Retention begins at successful upload completion.

`expires_at = uploaded_at + 30 days`

A daily scheduled backend job must:

1. Find videos where expires_at has passed.
2. Delete the MP4 object from R2.
3. Delete the thumbnail object from R2.
4. Mark the MySQL record EXPIRED or DELETED.
5. Reconcile any partially completed deletion.
6. Record errors for administrator review.

Deletion must be idempotent.

If object deletion succeeds but the database update fails, the next scheduled run must safely complete the database update.

Consider an R2 lifecycle rule at approximately 32 days as a secondary safety mechanism, not as the primary database synchronization mechanism.

Expired QR links must immediately become inaccessible through the API, even if the object has not yet been physically removed.

