# 15. Android 6.0.1/API 23 Compatibility

The physical target is Android 6.0.1/API 23.

Do not assume Android 16/API 36 runtime behavior.

Required changes:

- Add INTERNET permission.
- Add ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE permission.
- Retain the existing camera and other necessary permissions.
- Do not add RECORD_AUDIO unless the actual recording architecture requires microphone input.
- Use API23-compatible HTTP libraries.
- Use streaming upload requests.
- Avoid unsupported Android APIs.
- Verify TLS connectivity on the actual device.
- Verify the selected WorkManager version supports minSdk 23.
- If background scheduling is unsuitable, use a persistent upload service appropriate for the kiosk's operating mode.
- Preserve the existing foreground recording and processing safeguards.
- Keep upload operations away from the main thread.

The application must not depend on:
- Android 8+ notification channels
- Android 10+ scoped storage behavior
- Android 11+ package visibility
- Android 12+ mutable PendingIntent flags
- Android 13+ notification permission
- Android 14+ foreground service type requirements

Guard newer APIs with appropriate SDK checks.

Do not change the project's minSdk below or above the actual hardware requirement without explicit justification.

# 16. Backend Technology and Hosting Constraints

The Hostinger plan is currently unknown.

Default implementation recommendation:

- PHP 8.x REST API
- PDO for MySQL
- AWS SDK for PHP for R2 S3-compatible operations and presigned URLs
- Hostinger Cron Jobs for expiration and reconciliation
- Static or lightweight PHP-based responsive viewing page

Use the existing Node.js/Express backend only if the actual hosting subscription supports the required Node.js deployment and runtime.

Do not assume Node.js is available on every Hostinger plan.

Before implementation, verify:
- Hosting plan name
- PHP version and required extensions
- Composer dependency deployment
- MySQL availability
- Cron availability
- Domain/subdomain configuration
- HTTPS certificate
- Hostinger's rules for the intended application and traffic

The backend must not process, transcode, or proxy large video files. Android handles video processing, and R2 handles media storage and delivery.

# 17. Security and Privacy

Security requirements:

- R2 bucket must remain private.
- R2 secret credentials must remain server-side.
- Device API credentials must be revocable.
- QR tokens must be unguessable.
- Store token hashes rather than raw QR tokens.
- Use short-lived presigned upload and playback URLs.
- Validate all metadata.
- Enforce file-size limits.
- Verify MIME type and object existence.
- Rate-limit public API access.
- Protect administrator endpoints.
- Never expose database credentials in Android.
- Never expose cloud API secrets in the public website.
- Use HTTPS.
- Avoid storing guest names or personal details unless explicitly required.

Display a privacy notice before recording that explains:
- Videos are recorded on the kiosk.
- Videos are uploaded to online storage when internet is available.
- QR links allow anyone possessing them to view and download the associated video.
- Videos are automatically deleted after 30 days.
- Administrators can remove videos earlier.

Record the guest's acknowledgment in the local session/event flow, consistent with the existing guest privacy notice.

# 18. Error Handling and Recovery Matrix

| Failure | Expected response |
|---|---|
| Invalid device token | Reject request and notify admin |
| Event not active | Reject new upload initialization |
| File too large | Reject before upload |
| Unsupported MIME type | Reject upload |
| R2 PUT failure | Preserve local file and retry |
| Presigned URL expiration | Request a new URL |
| Missing R2 object | Do not mark AVAILABLE |
| Incorrect object size | Keep pending and report error |
| QR token revoked | Show unavailable page |
| QR link expired | Show expired page |
| R2 deletion failure | Retry during next cleanup |
| MySQL unavailable | Preserve local state |
| Database migration failure | Do not delete existing data |
| Local storage critical | Block new recording and alert admin |

All operations that can be retried must be idempotent.

Use a stable local video identifier and server-side idempotency keys to prevent duplicate cloud records.

